{"step_index":800,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T18:35:30Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T20:35:30+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T20:35:30+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///tmp/agyhome.NWyWnj/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/7841936c-55c7-41c5-92b8-9773cb8ee932/.system_generated/logs/transcript.jsonl`\nTotal Lines: 167\nTotal Bytes: 229741\nShowing lines 1 to 167\nContent truncated: showing bytes 40000-86080 of 229741. To see more, call this tool again with the same line range and ContentOffset=86080.\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: ions**\\n\\nIf the Roman domination number ($\\\\gamma_R$) can be at most 3, the graph's radius must be at most 2. Consider a Roman dominating function with total weight 4. This implies two vertices with value 2. Examine\\n<truncated 190 bytes>\\ns requires careful consideration of the adjacency between these two vertices. Specifically, if the vertices are adjacent to each other, the eccentricity from one can be at most 2. If the vertices are not adjacent to each other, a shortest path must exist to satisfy connectivity.\\n\\n\\n**Investigating Graph Radius Implications**\\n\\nIf a potential counterexample exists, specifically a graph with $rad(G) = 3$ and $\\\\gamma_R = 4$, then $rad(G) + 1 \\\\le \\\\gamma_R$ holds. The analysis focuses on Roman dominating functions and their impact on graph radius. Determining whether a graph of radius 3 can be Roman dominated by weight 4 is essential. The core question is whether radius 3 is achievable given the constraints of a Roman dominating function with weight 4.\\n\\n\\n**Analyzing Cycle Graphs and Domination**\\n\\nConsidering whether a cycle graph $C_n$ could serve as a counterexample, examining the relationship between $rad(C_n)$ and $\\\\gamma_R(C_n)$. The cycle graph's radius is $\\\\lfloor n/2 \\\\rfloor$ and the Roman domination number is $\\\\lceil 2n/3 \\\\rceil$. Comparing these expressions for various n values confirms the inequality $\\\\lceil 2n/3 \\\\rceil \\\\ge \\\\lfloor n/2 \\\\rfloor + 1$.\\n\\n**Exploring Radius and Roman Domination**\\n\\nThe investigation now focuses on if there exists a graph where $rad + 1 > \\\\gamma_R$. Considering the cases when $\\\\gamma_R$ equals 2, 3, or 4. If $\\\\gamma_R = 4$, can $rad = 4$? If the radius is 4, then the diameter is at least 4. Can two 2s dominate a graph of radius 4?\\n\\n\\n**Determining Graph Diameter and Radius**\\n\\nAssuming any vertex is adjacent to $u_1$ or $u_2$, and connectedness implies an edge between neighborhoods, a path between arbitrary vertices is at most length 5, so the diameter is at most 5.  The radius is at most $\\\\lfloor \\\\text{diam}/2 \\\\rfloor$. The eccentricity from a vertex in $N[u_1]$ is at most 3, thus the radius is at most 3.\\n\\nConsidering the case of one 2 and two 1s, it is explored how the distance between added vertices impacts radius. It is also found all vertices in $N(u)$ are distance 1 from $u$.\\n\\n\\n\\n\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"search_web\",\"args\":{\"query\":\"\\\"\\\\\\\"roman domination\\\\\\\" radius graph\\\"\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Searching the web\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Search roman domination and radius\\\"\"}}],\"truncated_fields\":[\"thinking\"]}\n2: {\"step_index\":31,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:30:13Z\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"run_command\",\"args\":{\"CommandLine\":\"\\\"python3 -c \\\\\\\"print('Python available')\\\\\\\"\\\"\",\"Cwd\":\"\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9\\\"\",\"WaitMsBeforeAsync\":\"5000\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Running command\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Test python availability\\\"\"}}]}\n3: {\"step_index\":32,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"GENERIC\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:30:14Z\",\"content\":\"Created At: 2026-09-11T20:30:14+02:00\\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T20:30:14+02:00\\n\\nThe command exited with code 0.\\nOutput:\\nPython available\\r\\n\\n\"}\n4: {\"step_index\":33,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:30:14Z\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"run_command\",\"args\":{\"CommandLine\":\"\\\"python3 -c \\\\\\\"import networkx as nx; print(nx.__version__)\\\\\\\"\\\"\",\"Cwd\":\"\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9\\\"\",\"WaitMsBeforeAsync\":\"5000\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Running command\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Test networkx\\\"\"}}]}\n5: {\"step_index\":34,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"GENERIC\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:30:24Z\",\"content\":\"Created At: 2026-09-11T20:30:24+02:00\\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T20:30:24+02:00\\n\\nThe command exited with code 1.\\nOutput:\\nTraceback (most recent call last):\\r\\n  File \\\"<string>\\\", line 1, in <module>\\r\\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'networkx'\\r\\n\\n\"}\n6: {\"step_index\":35,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:30:24Z\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"run_command\",\"args\":{\"CommandLine\":\"\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/.venv/bin/python -c \\\\\\\"import networkx as nx; print(nx.__version__)\\\\\\\"\\\"\",\"Cwd\":\"\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9\\\"\",\"WaitMsBeforeAsync\":\"5000\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Running command\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Test venv python networkx\\\"\"}}]}\n7: {\"step_index\":36,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"GENERIC\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:30:25Z\",\"content\":\"Created At: 2026-09-11T20:30:25+02:00\\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T20:30:25+02:00\\n\\nThe command exited with code 0.\\nOutput:\\n3.6.1\\r\\n\\n\"}\n8: {\"step_index\":37,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:30:25Z\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"run_command\",\"args\":{\"CommandLine\":\"\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/.venv/bin/python -c '\\\\nimport networkx as nx\\\\nfrom itertools import product\\\\n\\\\ndef roman_domination_number(G):\\\\n    # MIP or brute force\\\\n    nodes = list(G.nodes())\\\\n    n = len(nodes)\\\\n    # Using integer programming or simple search\\\\n    # Since n <= 9, branch and bound or ILP\\\\n    best = 2 * n\\\\n    # We can use scipy or simple recursive search\\\\n    def search(idx, current_f, current_weight):\\\\n        nonlocal best\\\\n        if current_weight >= best:\\\\n            return\\\\n        if idx == n:\\\\n            # check validity\\\\n            valid = True\\\\n            for u in nodes:\\\\n                if current_f[u] == 0:\\\\n                    if not any(current_f[v] == 2 for v in G.neighbors(u)):\\\\n                        valid = False\\\\n                        break\\\\n            if valid:\\\\n                best = current_weight\\\\n            return\\\\n        u = nodes[idx]\\\\n        for val in [2, 1, 0]:\\\\n            current_f[u] = val\\\\n            search(idx + 1, current_f, current_weight + val)\\\\n    search(0, {}, 0)\\\\n    return best\\\\n\\\\nprint(\\\\\\\"Testing...\\\\\\\")\\\\ncount = 0\\\\nfor n in range(2, 8):\\\\n    for G in nx.graph_atlas_g():\\\\n        if G.number_of_nodes() == n and nx.is_connected(G):\\\\n            count += 1\\\\n            rad = nx.radius(G)\\\\n            rd = roman_domination_number(G)\\\\n            if rad + 1 > rd:\\\\n                print(f\\\\\\\"COUNTEREXAMPLE: n={n}, rad={rad}, rd={rd}, edges={list(G.edges())}\\\\\\\")\\\\n                exit(0)\\\\nprint(f\\\\\\\"Tested {count} connected graphs from atlas. None violated rad + 1 <= rd.\\\\\\\")\